+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+See proposal.md (Why). Current state, observed in the repository:
+
+- `src/cache.ts:10` stores entries in a module-level `Map<string, CacheEntry>` keyed by widget id; nothing ever removes an entry except `invalidate` (`:18`) and `clear` (`:22`).
+- `src/cache.ts:12` `get(id)` reads by id; `render(widget)` (`:30`) returns `get(widget.id)` when present, so a widget whose `props` changed is served its old HTML.
+- `src/render.ts:3` wraps every widget's output in `<div data-id="…">…</div>`, so a rendered entry is never shorter than 21 bytes.
+- `package.json` has no dependencies, no scripts and no test runner; there is no `test/` directory. `bun` 1.4.4 and `node` 26 are on the machine; `bun test` runs TypeScript directly.
+- The cache is a module singleton used by the dashboard shell; tests share that singleton and must reset it between cases.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+- Bound the cache by the UTF-8 byte size of the HTML it holds, evicting least recently used entries (specs/widget-render-cache/spec.md).
+- Stop serving HTML rendered from outdated props (taken follow-up).
+- Keep the existing exported signatures (`get`, `has`, `invalidate`, `clear`, `size`, `render`, `CacheEntry`).
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+- Time-based expiry; `storedAt` stays informational.
+- Counting the props fingerprint against the byte budget (see Risks).
+- Escaping `w.id` in `src/render.ts` (the other roadmap follow-up; this change does not touch that file).
+- Persisting the cache or sharing it across tabs/processes.
+
+## Decisions
+
+**D1. Size-bounded LRU from `lru-cache`.** The store becomes `new LRUCache<string, CacheEntry>({ maxSize, sizeCalculation })`, where `sizeCalculation` returns the UTF-8 byte length of `entry.html`. `maxEntrySize` keeps its default (equal to `maxSize`). Behaviour verified with a scratch install of 11.5.3 outside this repository:
+- `get` marks the entry most recently used; `has` does not (doc comment on `LRUCache.has` in the package's `src/index.ts`: "without updating the recency of use").
+- Setting an entry larger than `maxEntrySize` stores nothing and deletes the existing entry for that key, leaving the others — exactly the "Output larger than the budget is not cached" requirement.
+- `calculatedSize` is the running byte total; `size` the entry count.
+- `sizeCalculation` returning 0 throws; unreachable here because rendered HTML is at least 21 bytes (Context).
+Alternative: keep the `Map` and rely on its insertion order (delete + re-set on use, evict from `keys().next()`) with a hand-kept byte counter — rejected, see Build vs. adopt.
+
+**D2. Module API.** Exported additions in `src/cache.ts`:
+- `DEFAULT_MAX_BYTES = 1_048_576`.
+- `totalBytes(): number` → `calculatedSize`.
+- `setMaxBytes(bytes: number): void` — throws `RangeError` unless `Number.isSafeInteger(bytes) && bytes > 0`, before touching state. `lru-cache`'s `maxSize` is read-only, so it builds a new store with the new budget and re-inserts the old entries from `rentries()` (least to most recently used), which preserves recency order and lets the new store evict the oldest until the budget holds. The module-level binding then points at the new store.
+`get`, `has`, `invalidate`, `clear` and `size` delegate to the store with unchanged signatures. Alternative: a `configure({ maxBytes })` options object — rejected, one option does not need an options bag.
+
+**D3. Props fingerprint in the entry.** `CacheEntry` gains `propsKey: string`, the `JSON.stringify(widget.props)` the HTML was rendered from. `render(widget)`:
+1. Compute the fingerprint; if `JSON.stringify` throws (cycle, BigInt) or returns `undefined`, delete any entry for `widget.id` and return `renderWidget(widget)` without storing.
+2. `const hit = store.get(widget.id)`; return `hit.html` when `hit.propsKey === fingerprint`.
+3. Otherwise render, store `{ html, storedAt, propsKey }` (replacing the stale entry), return the HTML.
+The map key stays the widget id, so `get(id)`/`invalidate(id)` keep working and a widget never occupies budget with several prop variants. Alternatives: key by `id + fingerprint` — rejected, stale variants would hold budget until evicted and `invalidate(id)` could not find them; compare `props` by reference — rejected, widgets that mutate `props` in place would still be served stale HTML.
+
+**D4. Tests with `bun test`.** One file `test/cache.test.ts` using `bun:test`; `beforeEach` calls `clear()` and `setMaxBytes(DEFAULT_MAX_BYTES)` so cases do not leak through the singleton. A helper builds a widget whose rendered HTML has an exact byte length (21 bytes of wrapper + id + body) and counts calls to its `render()`. `describe` blocks are named after the spec requirements so each task's Verify can select its block with `-t`.
+
+### Build vs. adopt: size-bounded LRU cache
+- Repository: none. Searched `src/cache.ts`, `src/render.ts`, `src/widget.ts` and `package.json` (no dependencies, no `node_modules`); `src/cache.ts:10` is an unbounded `Map` with no eviction.
+- Platform: `Map` iterates in insertion order (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Map), which gives an ordering primitive but no size accounting, budget or eviction; partial only.
+- Candidates: `lru-cache` — https://github.com/isaacs/node-lru-cache; newest release 11.5.3, 2026-09-18 (deps.dev); licence BlueOak-1.0.0; maintained; adopt: `maxSize` + `sizeCalculation` + `maxEntrySize` cover the byte budget, LRU order, oversized-entry rejection and byte total. `quick-lru` — https://github.com/sindresorhus/quick-lru; newest release 7.3.0, 2025-10-10 (deps.dev); licence MIT; maintained; reject, gap: `maxSize` counts entries only, no per-entry size calculation, so it cannot bound bytes.
+- Decision: adopt `lru-cache` 11.5.3.
+
+### Build vs. adopt: UTF-8 byte length
+- Repository: none; searched the same paths as above.
+- Platform: `TextEncoder.prototype.encode` returns the UTF-8 bytes (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/TextEncoder/encode), available in browsers, Node and Bun.
+- Candidates: not searched, covered above.
+- Decision: adopt the platform `TextEncoder` (one module-level instance; one encode per stored entry).
+
+### Build vs. adopt: props change detection
+- Repository: none; searched the same paths as above.
+- Platform: `JSON.stringify` (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/JSON/stringify) serializes plain props deterministically for the same key order and throws on cycles and BigInt, which D3 uses as the bypass signal.
+- Candidates: not searched, covered above.
+- Decision: adopt the platform `JSON.stringify`; the fingerprint is compared for equality only and never parsed.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Props whose only change is in a function, `undefined` or symbol value serialize identically, so the old HTML is served] → Widget props are data in this dashboard; recorded as an accepted residual risk in gate.md.
+- [Same props with a different key order produce a different fingerprint] → Causes one extra render, never stale output.
+- [The fingerprint string is held in memory but not counted against the byte budget] → The budget bounds HTML as the spec defines; very large props raise real memory above the budget. Accepted residual risk.
+- [`JSON.stringify` of large props runs on every `render` call, including hits] → Cost is proportional to props size, far below a widget render; measured in F1's happy-path row.
+- [`setMaxBytes` rebuilds the store, O(entries)] → Called rarely (configuration), 40 widgets on the reference board.
+- [New runtime dependency] → Single package with no dependencies of its own (`npm view lru-cache@11.5.3 dependencies` is empty) and a dedicated `browser` export, so the dashboard's browser bundle does not pull Node-only modules; version pinned through `bun.lock`.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+No data migration: the cache is in-memory and starts empty. Deploy with the new dependency installed (`bun install`). Rollback: revert the change commits; the old `Map` cache needs nothing from the new one.
